Yes—and your last thought fits it. A style house makes uncommon choices feel normal inside its own world. The outside world may find them unusual; within the house, they’re coherent, expected, and allowed.

AI makes production cheaper, so the scarce thing shifts toward taste with continuity: knowing what belongs, what doesn’t, and how a sensibility should show up across a package, a film, a product, a voice, even a way of behaving. The style house owns that grammar and uses AI as its workshop.

I can imagine small, internet-native houses doing this across whole worlds—not only fashion. VEYR could be a design house; Wild Hawg already has the beginnings of a house style; Pantheon could help map and generate the underlying grammars. The opportunity is to make something recognizable without repeating the same look: a family resemblance, with room for surprise.